Subject: winepulse: Stereo Mix, and loopback that follows the default output

Recording programs (OBS, TikTok Live Studio, Discord, Teams) look for a
"Stereo Mix" capture device to record what the computer plays, as most
Windows sound cards offer. Add one ("Mixage stéréo" in French). It records
the monitor of the default output through a PipeWire stream with
stream.capture.sink and no target, so PipeWire moves it to the new
default output when that changes, Bluetooth headsets included.

Loopback capture (AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_LOOPBACK) on the default output
uses the same stream, so it follows too; before, it stayed on the output
that was the default when the stream opened. Loopback on a given output
still records that output's monitor.

The sound server's "Monitor of ..." sources no longer show up as
microphones; they stay known for loopback.

Capture streams also stop losing sound silently: the server sends a
whole graph cycle at a time (1024 frames or more with Bluetooth or in a
virtual machine), which could be more than the buffer the program asked
for, and the server dropped what did not fit before it was read. The
server buffer is no longer capped, and the capture buffer holds at least
100 ms (Windows may also give more than asked); a program that falls
behind still gets the newest sound, with the gap flagged.
